I think most 'terrible luck' problems result either from player 
taking risks (quaffing fountains etc.) or from powerful wands 
lying around.  While the first can't be helped, I think the second 
would have an easy solution:  Give each wand a min_level 
property, such that they can only be created on levels with 
level >= min_level.  Trouble with this 'simple solution' 
(apart from the maelstrom of flaming outcries against 
this idea which would come forth) is of course that this 
would be a rather deep-level change in the source, changing 
general object property listings and such.  OTOH, it would 
change both problems quite well; against the fountain-drinkers 
it doesn't work, but this is not the major problem.  Poly traps 
are the only other source of 'terrible luck' I can imagine; maybe 
here the solution would be to randomly change the monster type 
into something with the same amount of HP as the original creature 
or below.  The obvious consequence would of course be that you 
can't pet-polymorph your pet to something nasty as easily as 
now.  

Having said that, I must admit I'm totally against ruling out 
bad luck in early game by such means.  Sometimes it is reassuring 
to know that *this* *time*, it was really not your fault, but the 
cruel RNG who went beserk.  

